 "nodes": [
  {
   "id": 1,
   "type": "note",
   "x": -380,
   "y": 20,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "MOUSE CORTICAL COLUMN. Four layer bands stacked by real cortical depth, filled at population proportions from the reference microcircuit (Potjans and Diesmann 2014). Layer 5 pyramids are intrinsically bursting, a tenth of layer 2/3 chatter, and every layer splits its interneurons between fast spiking and low threshold spiking.",
    "width": 340
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": 2,
   "type": "note",
   "x": -380,
   "y": 170,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "The connect node carries the verified 8 by 8 population pair table, so each layer to layer probability reproduces the published value rather than a distance guess. A pulse train drives layer 4, the input layer, through a geometry mask.",
    "width": 340
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": 3,
   "type": "note",
   "x": -380,
   "y": 320,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "Background drive is tuned per layer. Spontaneous rates with the thalamic pulse off, against the de Kock and Sakmann 2009 bands: layer 2/3 excitatory 0.34 Hz (band 0.2 to 1), layer 4 1.18 (0.5 to 3), layer 5 2.30 (2 to 4), layer 6 0.92 (0.3 to 2), every inhibitory population above its excitatory partner, and population Fano per thousand 0.2, which is asynchronous. The validation battery gates all of that.",
    "width": 340
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
